>
  <p>
    Superficial fungal infection — tinea, commonly called ringworm — produces itchy, scaly patches
    with a raised advancing edge and relative clearing in the centre. It spreads outward, and it
    spreads between people and through shared clothing, towels and bedding.
  </p>

  <p>
    In India over the last decade these infections have become markedly harder to treat. Wider
    areas, longer courses, more recurrence. Two things explain most of it: easy availability of
    combination creams containing potent steroids, and incomplete courses of treatment.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it appears</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tinea corporis</strong> — body, trunk and limbs</li>
    <li><strong>Tinea cruris</strong> — groin and inner thighs, very common in hot humid months</li>
    <li><strong>Tinea pedis</strong> — feet and between the toes</li>
    <li><strong>Tinea capitis</strong> — scalp, mostly in children; always needs oral treatment</li>
    <li><strong>Onychomycosis</strong> — nails; thickened, discoloured, crumbling. Needs months of treatment</li>
    <li><strong>Pityriasis versicolor</strong> — pale or light brown flat patches on the chest and back. A different organism and a different treatment</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is confirmed</h2>

  <p>
    A <strong>KOH mount</strong> takes a few minutes. A scraping from the edge of the patch is
    examined under the microscope, and fungal filaments are either there or they are not. It is
    cheap, it is quick, and skipping it is why so many rashes are treated for months with the
    wrong cream.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where the infection has been altered by steroid use, or has failed appropriate treatment, a
    fungal culture identifies the species and guides which oral agent to use.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment</h2>

  <h3>Topical</h3>
  <p>
    A plain antifungal — never a combination containing a steroid — applied to the patch and a
    margin of normal skin beyond its edge, continued for a period after it looks clear.
  </p>

  <h3>Oral</h3>
  <p>
    Terbinafine or itraconazole for widespread, recurrent, scalp or nail disease. Course length
    depends on the site and extent, and liver function may be checked depending on the agent and
    duration.
  </p>

  <h3>The part that actually prevents recurrence</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Treat everyone in the household who is affected, at the same time</li>
    <li>Hot-wash clothing, towels and bedlinen; dry in sunlight where possible</li>
    <li>Loose cotton clothing; change out of damp clothes promptly</li>
    <li>Dry carefully in the groin, under the arms and between the toes</li>
    <li>Do not share towels, combs or footwear</li>
    <li>Complete the full course even after it looks clear</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note note-warn">
    <h3>Combination creams</h3>
    <p>
      Creams mixing an antifungal, an antibiotic and a potent steroid are sold widely and used for
      almost any itchy rash. They relieve itching within a day, which is why people trust them, and
      they allow fungus to spread wider and deeper while changing its appearance so the diagnosis
      is missed. If you have one at home, bring it rather than discarding it — knowing exactly what
      was used changes the treatment plan.
    </p>
  </div>
